{
  "id": "80609",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "The Solubility Product",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The preservation of medical laboratory blood samples, mining of sea water for magnesium, formulation of over-the-counter medicines such as milk of magnesia and antacids, and treating the hard water in your home’s water supply are just a few of the many tasks that involve controlling the equilibrium between a slightly soluble ionic solid and an aqueous solution of its ions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "35p7pand44utd2j",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In some cases, we want to prevent dissolution from occurring. Tooth decay, for example, occurs when the calcium hydroxylapatite, which has the formula Ca"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "5"
              },
              {
                "text": "(PO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "(OH), in our teeth dissolves. The "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "dissolution"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The process of dissolving."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1971900852",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1214557394",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " process is aided when bacteria in our mouths feast on the sugars in our diets to produce lactic acid, which reacts with the hydroxide ions in the calcium hydroxylapatite. Preventing the dissolution prevents the decay. On the other hand, sometimes we want a substance to dissolve. We want the calcium carbonate in a chewable antacid to dissolve because the CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2−"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions produced in this process help soothe an upset stomach."
              }
            ],
            "id": "r9kzj8bnmk8x0ut",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In this module, we find out how we can control the dissolution of a slightly soluble ionic solid by the application of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Le Châtelier’s principle"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An empirical framework stating that when a system at equilibrium is subjected to a stress (such as a change in concentration, pressure, or temperature), the system will adjust in a way that counteracts the stress and reestablishes equilibrium."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2321215289",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4216130832",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". We also learn how to use the equilibrium constant of the reaction to determine the concentration of ions present in a saturated solution."
              }
            ],
            "id": "60mzcgxr18wksnq",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ppppv2ivhbgul2f",
                "text": "Solubility Product Constant ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "id": "w23zfgqhowpe6ur",
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "id": "cojlvt3d6hyi8jf",
                "sub": true,
                "text": "sp"
              },
              {
                "id": "k5bd1u1pjgikyus",
                "text": ")"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ca28d2m3ky0s670",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Silver chloride is what’s known as a "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "sparingly soluble ionic solid"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Recall from the solubility guidelines in an earlier unit that halides of Ag"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " are not normally soluble. However, when we add an excess of solid AgCl to water, it dissolves to a small extent and produces a mixture consisting of a very dilute solution of Ag"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " and Cl"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions in equilibrium with undissolved silver chloride:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "urlngz9r87oyh4g",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "bemkw0h8fe41nzq",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "iv6qtp7ssvxm5sf",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{AgCl}(s)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\overset{\\mathrm{dissolution}}{\\underset{\\mathrm{precipitation}}\\rightleftharpoons}\\;\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm{Ag}^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Cl}^-(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "This equilibrium, like other equilibria, is dynamic; some of the solid AgCl continues to dissolve, but at the same time, Ag"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " and Cl"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "–"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions in the solution combine to produce an equal amount of the solid. At equilibrium, the opposing processes have equal rates."
              }
            ],
            "id": "24kjtghbg53zy5m",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "Two beakers are shown with a bidirectional arrow between them. Both beakers are just over half filled with a clear, colorless liquid. The beaker on the left shows a cubic structure composed of alternating green and slightly larger grey spheres. Evenly distributed in the region outside, 11 space filling models are shown. These are each composed of a central red sphere with two smaller white spheres attached in a bent arrangement. In the beaker on the right, the green and grey spheres are no longer connected in a cubic structure. Nine green spheres, 10 grey spheres, and 11 red and white molecules are evenly mixed and distributed throughout the liquid in the beaker.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Silver chloride is a sparingly soluble ionic solid. When it is added to water, it dissolves slightly and produces a mixture consisting of a very dilute solution of Ag"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " and Cl"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "–"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " ions in equilibrium with undissolved silver chloride."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "xzn1cu6dc10ch0t",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "e1dsi0mew069k86",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/7e/7ebd9aad3077cc23cb6ce735f0d7462f/CNX_Chem_15_01_AgCl.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The equilibrium constant for the equilibrium between a slightly soluble ionic solid and a solution of its ions is called the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "solubility product constant ("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "sp"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ")"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Equilibrium constant for the dissolution of an ionic compound."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1801510291",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4209788895",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " of the solid. We use an ion’s concentration as an approximation of its activity in a dilute solution. For silver chloride, at equilibrium:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3rmy6w837kv57mt",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "fkfkkhyaodczcfr",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "sr0047dg4es8ec0",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{AgCl}(s)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm{Ag}^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Cl}^-(aq)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;K_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\;=\\;\\lbrack\\mathrm{Ag}^+(aq)\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm{Cl}^-(aq)\\rbrack",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Note that the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "sp"
              },
              {
                "text": " expression does not contain a term in the denominator for the concentration of the reactant, AgCl(s). According to the guidelines for deriving mass-action expressions described in Module 4, only gases and solutes are represented. Solids and liquids are assigned concentration values of 1 and thus do not appear in equilibrium constant expressions. Therefore, [AgCl] does not appear in the expression for "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "sp"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "azmur79h23iyoa9",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Some common solubility products are listed in the table below according to their "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "sp"
              },
              {
                "text": " values. A more extensive list of solubility products is found here: "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Solubility Products"
                  }
                ],
                "href": "/course/link/iz1ln_solubility_products_k4ulz",
                "idref": "80605",
                "target": "new",
                "type": "a"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Each of these equilibrium constants is much smaller than 1 because the compounds listed are only slightly soluble. A small "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "sp"
              },
              {
                "text": " represents a system in which the equilibrium lies to the left, so relatively few hydrated ions would be present in a saturated solution."
              }
            ],
            "id": "lv3e2vxijyfpmfz",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "k5v4zzapu3awic9",
                "text": "Common Solubility Products by Decreasing Equilibrium Constants"
              }
